  "Full API spec": "C++17 To-Do List App Public API and Contracts\n\ninclude/Task.h\n- enum class Priority { Low, Medium, High };\n- struct Task {\n  std::uint64_t id; std::string title; std::string description; std::string due_date; Priority priority; std::vector<std::string> tags; bool completed;\n  static std::string Trim(const std::string& s);\n  static std::string NormalizeTag(const std::string& tag);\n  static std::vector<std::string> NormalizeTags(const std::vector<std::string>& tags);\n  static bool IsValidTitle(const std::string& title);\n};\nSemantics: Trim removes leading/trailing ASCII whitespace. NormalizeTag applies Trim then ASCII lowercase; returns possibly empty if tag trims to empty. NormalizeTags maps NormalizeTag over input, removes empties, sorts ascending lexicographically, deduplicates deterministically, returns stable vector. IsValidTitle returns true if after Trim the title is non-empty.\nHeader self-containment: must include <cstdint>, <string>, <vector> directly.\n\ninclude/Date.h\n- class DateRules {\n  public: static bool is_valid_iso_date(const std::string& date);\n          static int compare_dates(const std::string& a, const std::string& b);\n};\nSemantics: is_valid_iso_date returns true only for exact YYYY-MM-DD with digits, year 0000-9999 allowed, month 01-12, day 01-31 with month-specific and leap-year checks. Empty string is permitted as a non-date and is treated as invalid for validation but allowed by TaskManager. compare_dates imposes total order: if both valid, compare by date ascending (earlier < later). If one empty and one valid, empty sorts after valid: compare_dates(\"\", valid) > 0; compare_dates(valid, \"\") < 0; compare_dates(\"\", \"\") == 0. If invalid non-empty strings appear (should not via TaskManager), behavior is deterministic: treat invalid as greater than valid and equal among invalids (implementation ensures stability). Header must include <string> directly.\n\ninclude/Query.h\n- enum class SortOrder { ByDueThenPriority, ByTitle }; // Global, not nested\n- struct FilterOptions {\n  std::optional<bool> completed; std::optional<Priority> min_priority; std::optional<std::string> due_before; std::optional<std::string> due_after; std::vector<std::string> require_tags;\n};\n- class Query {\n  public: static FilterOptions WithTags(const FilterOptions& base, const std::vector<std::string>& tags);\n          static bool matches(const Task& t, const FilterOptions& f);\n          static bool less_due_priority(const Task& a, const Task& b);\n          static bool less_title(const Task& a, const Task& b);\n};\nSemantics: WithTags returns a copy of base with require_tags normalized via Task::NormalizeTags. matches returns true iff all active filters pass: completed if set; min_priority if set (accepts tasks with priority >= min); due_before if set (task due_date must be valid and strictly earlier than due_before; empty task date fails due_before); due_after if set (task due_date must be valid and strictly later than due_after; empty fails due_after); require_tags treated as set-inclusion after normalization (task must contain all required tags; comparison on normalized forms). Ordering: less_due_priority compares earlier valid due_date first using DateRules::compare_dates, treating empty due dates as greater; then higher Priority first (High > Medium > Low); then title ascending lexicographically; then id ascending for stability. less_title compares title ascending, then id ascending. Header must include <optional>, <string>, <vector>, \"Date.h\", and \"Task.h\"; SortOrder must remain a global enum (TaskManager uses unqualified name).\n\ninclude/TaskStore.h\n- class ITaskStore {\n  public: virtual ~ITaskStore() = default; virtual bool add(const Task& t) = 0; virtual bool update(const Task& t) = 0; virtual bool remove(std::uint64_t id) = 0; virtual Task* find(std::uint64_t id) = 0; virtual std::vector<Task> get_all() const = 0;\n};\n- class InMemoryTaskStore : public ITaskStore {\n  private: std::map<std::uint64_t, Task> tasks_;\n  public: bool add(const Task& t) override; bool update(const Task& t) override; bool remove(std::uint64_t id) override; Task* find(std::uint64_t id) override; std::vector<Task> get_all() const override;\n};\nSemantics: add inserts if id not present; update overwrites existing by id; remove deletes by id; find returns pointer to stored Task or nullptr; get_all returns defensive copy vector sorted by ascending id. Header must directly include <cstdint>, <map>, <vector>, and \"Task.h\"; it must include <map> before declaring tasks_.\n\ninclude/TaskManager.h\n- class TaskManager {\n  public: explicit TaskManager(ITaskStore& store);\n          std::uint64_t create_task(const std::string& title, const std::string& description, const std::string& due_date, Priority priority, const std::vector<std::string>& tags);\n          bool edit_task(std::uint64_t id, const std::string& title, const std::string& description, const std::string& due_date, Priority priority, const std::vector<std::string>& tags);\n          bool delete_task(std::uint64_t id);\n          bool set_completed(std::uint64_t id, bool completed);\n          std::vector<Task> list(const FilterOptions& filters, SortOrder order) const;\n          std::pair<std::size_t, std::size_t> summarize() const;\n  private: ITaskStore& store_; std::uint64_t next_id_;\n};\nSemantics: IDs start at 1 and increase monotonically per process. create_task validates title via Task::IsValidTitle and due_date via DateRules::is_valid_iso_date or allows empty due_date; throws std::invalid_argument on invalid title or non-empty invalid date. Stores tasks with completed=false and normalized tags (Task::NormalizeTags). edit_task revalidates title/date and updates existing task; returns false if id missing; throws std::invalid_argument on invalid inputs. delete_task returns false for missing id; true on success. set_completed returns false for missing id; true on success. list returns filtered + sorted copy of tasks per Query::matches and SortOrder: SortOrder::ByDueThenPriority uses Query::less_due_priority; SortOrder::ByTitle uses Query::less_title. summarize returns {completed_count, pending_count}. Header must include <cstddef>, <cstdint>, <string>, <utility>, <vector>, \"Query.h\", \"TaskStore.h\".\n\nsrc/Task.cpp\nImplements Task helpers exactly; includes \"Task.h\", <algorithm>, <cctype>.\n\nsrc/Date.cpp\nImplements DateRules; includes \"Date.h\" and <cctype>; helper functions (e.g., IsLeapYear) are declared/defined before any use; empty-date compare signs: compare_dates(\"\", valid) > 0; compare_dates(valid, \"\") < 0; compare_dates(\"\", \"\") == 0.\n\nsrc/Query.cpp\nImplements WithTags, matches, and comparators per above; includes \"Query.h\", <algorithm>, and must directly include <set>; uses std::set for tag inclusion checks.\n\nsrc/TaskStore.cpp\nImplements InMemoryTaskStore methods; includes \"TaskStore.h\" only.\n\nsrc/TaskManager.cpp\nImplements TaskManager; includes \"TaskManager.h\", <algorithm>, <stdexcept>; uses unqualified global SortOrder; throws std::invalid_argument as specified; returns false for missing ids as specified.\n\nsrc/Main.cpp\nNon-interactive demo: constructs InMemoryTaskStore and TaskManager, creates a few tasks, lists with both sort orders, prints a small summary, returns 0. No I/O beyond small prints; no loops.\n\ntests/test_main.cpp\nTest file includes <cassert>, <stdexcept>, <vector>, and \"TaskManager.h\"; defines exactly one int main() with three independent scoped blocks. The invalid-data block declares its own local existing_id as: const auto existing_id = manager.create_task(\"Existing\", \"\", \"\", Priority::Low, {}); It asserts exceptions with try/catch or assert throwing patterns; for vector comparisons inside assert, it first declares named expected vectors instead of braced initializers. It never includes .cpp files.\n\nAllowed include directions\n- Headers are self-contained (no reliance on transitive includes). Sources include their paired headers and any algorithm/utility standard headers they use as mandated above. Tests include only include/TaskManager.h. SortOrder remains global and is used unqualified everywhere.\n\nError behavior and determinism\n- All logic is pure/deterministic with no I/O, filesystem, network, threads, clocks, or randomness in core. Invalid create/edit inputs throw std::invalid_argument; missing ids for delete/set_completed return false. Sorting is deterministic with tie-breakers on title and id.",
